{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ pennsylvania \"gas tax\" \"state police\" (+ roads bridges \"road improvements\") (+ funding revenue dollars))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_43142227#3_72729488","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3270","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_43142227#3_72729488\" , \"end_char\": 2411.000000 , \"headings\": \"Pennsylvania’s gas tax highest in the U.S., so where is it going?<cover>Pennsylvania’s gas tax highest in the U.S., so where is it going?\" , \"segment\": \"The PSP funding is a sticking point. The rationale behind it is that troopers patrol the highways, but lawmakers are questioning whether there should be different sources for State Police funding. The gas tax revenue makes up a large portion of the PSP budget, and PennDOT would like to free up that funding for road improvements. “</cover>It allows us to plan in the long-term because we can count on this funding,” Richards said. “ We know it’s reliable, it’s coming, and it allows us to plan for these big projects.” Richards says the lower gas taxes in other states can mean less reliable funding, which can lead to project delays. “Many times it has to be redesigned, many times you have to go back and get environmental permits that have expired, and ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_43142227#2_72727978","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
