{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_350022295#4_649551864","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly states that federal, local, and mostly state governments diverted $21.3 billion from highway-user fees into non-highway programs, supporting diversion of transportation-related revenues. However, the document is primarily about comparing transport costs per passenger mile, not dedicated to state transportation-fund mismanagement.","learned":"A highway-user-fee framing can surface evidence that governments, including mostly states, divert road-user revenues into non-highway programs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3271","reason":"This query produced direct evidence of highway-user revenues diverted to non-highway programs; continuing may find more focused or state-specific documents using this terminology."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3271"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_350022295#5_649553142","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3271","rank":2,"snippet":"\": \"Transport Costs Per Passenger Mile Transport Costs Per Passenger Mile About The Antiplanner \" , \"segment\": \"Offsetting this, federal, local, and (mostly) <cover>state governments diverted $21.3 billion from highway user fees into non-highway programs. So the net subsidy was $17.9 billion. ( By comparison, transit received $30 billion in subsidies in 2005.) The highway subsidy works out to a subsidy of 0.38 cents per passenger mile. Added to the consumer cost and the total cost of driving is 22.4 cents per passenger mile — still a lot less than transit costs. What about the social costs of driving? Back in the 1990s, UC economist Mark Delucchi did a massive series of studies of these social costs, which you can download from the University of California Transportation Center. He summarized his work in an article in Access magazine, which is also published by the University of California Transportation Center. Many auto opponents have attributed extremely high social costs to autos and roads. </cover>After reviewing the literature, Delucchi concluded that most of these claims “rely on outdated, superficial, nongeneralizable, or otherwise inappropriate studies.”\" , \"start_char\": 3357."}}
